#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216953 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "f6884f33-97b6-502e-91c8-bdd3a4fc95d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21868" :question/problem "$$\nx^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216954 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "bac67ae0-dc3c-528c-9e07-7a81caa86db4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21869" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4.$ What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)?$" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216955 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/id #uuid "008cfc63-46d4-5e99-84a2-76df45a51001" :question/math-academy-id "e-21870" :question/problem "$$\ny''+4y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=-3.$ Use Abel's identity to conclude whether $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent on $\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{-4}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=-3$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -3 & =C{e}^{-8} \\\\ C & =-3{e}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=-3e^{8}e^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSince $-3e^{8}e^{-4x}\\neq 0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we conclude that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/assets/1e26515be7d7b9ecf5cd5e1464989f6d90098247bdd95dfae75fd2545fd9bd18.png)\n\nSo, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/assets/6397adc2a5f64c899725f85822627697055696c0898ffb6e2ee1d2417e802caa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1421ed9-8eac-58d0-9737-f173a3cc9186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216626} {:answer/id #uuid "f59ca442-68a0-5f1e-84eb-539db5ee393d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216627} {:answer/id #uuid "107bd02b-27ad-5ea4-a076-9b6f06cf6efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− xW(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216628} {:answer/id #uuid "357b3320-1e4f-5e24-88b0-fa310da7de21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sin (x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216629} {:answer/id #uuid "60bebb95-c94e-5bbf-a1bf-ec34b48e2b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\sin (x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1421ed9-8eac-58d0-9737-f173a3cc9186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216626} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b763af-91e4-597f-acba-401fdd402194" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2c11932-8453-50ce-aa58-370fc8f388e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342709" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}W(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4af9ea7-f7b2-53d5-a9dd-d92551f659dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216644} {:answer/id #uuid "f0260b6a-c3fa-592e-b10a-d1e774416ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216645} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4c6d4c-c19b-5df3-96e1-037e181fa722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216646} {:answer/id #uuid "59e064a2-0228-5c52-afdb-5d0c2c8c7e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59e064a2-0228-5c52-afdb-5d0c2c8c7e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216647} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9f3cbb-3b99-5d82-977b-f8700df47fa0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216643} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2332cbc2-c380-52cb-a351-45c5ded32e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367953} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd55b4e-6889-5254-9250-7cadc86fa9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367954} {:answer/id #uuid "b051053d-d286-531a-876b-d04e53a1db5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367955} {:answer/id #uuid "c38e73e2-aece-5726-acc3-fb6e1b3a841c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2332cbc2-c380-52cb-a351-45c5ded32e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367953} :answer-field/id #uuid "9612ebd1-4dee-53f1-aa10-a05b110b82a6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367952} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e218f341-d01c-52b2-8023-52d230848b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367958} {:answer/id #uuid "15418217-b5a9-5169-a102-869b12624d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367959} {:answer/id #uuid "197784a1-20a0-5079-9cd2-1a8bd2e757c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e218f341-d01c-52b2-8023-52d230848b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367958} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe235196-f225-5d31-b709-24f898f4e0ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ac49bd9-9719-507d-b361-709e747ff5e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-342724" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083d2188-9a75-5a73-820a-67b44f40dc68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-3x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367963} {:answer/id #uuid "b88d89ce-770a-511c-bc9f-31dd7f812de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3xW(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367964} {:answer/id #uuid "2d54a241-9400-50be-8fd4-59746463cc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(x+1)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367965} {:answer/id #uuid "87802a5c-8a88-5935-be4c-a6c289af29d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367966} {:answer/id #uuid "588434b2-7206-5a14-b78b-728d6428c83a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "083d2188-9a75-5a73-820a-67b44f40dc68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-3x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367963} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd1afa58-adba-5913-820b-1c6661399dc9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f6435cd-574f-5efd-a976-2c2c34c55725" :question/math-academy-id "q-343255" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+(3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)){y}^{′}+(x+1)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-3x)W(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c865006-2c69-526a-941d-108b75e72e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367970} {:answer/id #uuid "40d9e584-68ce-5ae7-b4bc-105dfd87aa5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367971} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ddf590-365e-59a1-8ece-dce00aa27200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367972} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7fa634-3581-541f-8e7a-ca6b0b174c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367973} {:answer/id #uuid "2abcf027-e933-519a-94d9-043a1fa284a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2ddf590-365e-59a1-8ece-dce00aa27200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367972} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ffd9db-eaaf-5f89-96f5-487b82c86206" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14454f59-3ca6-50f2-ab4a-60ef1b368cb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343256" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+{e}^{x}{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{x}W(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4b0f91-6c58-56d6-899f-2090b42078ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367977} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3236bf-bed0-51d4-b1ac-836d0f3a0d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367978} {:answer/id #uuid "f99811fe-31d0-5e53-830f-25c34fd00e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367979} {:answer/id #uuid "28682822-79b0-592e-a7fe-8dc29da72d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367980} {:answer/id #uuid "9572e7ff-099d-55fd-875d-51b4505493fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9572e7ff-099d-55fd-875d-51b4505493fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367981} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1efcc66-0d3d-5b73-b479-299c8e5057db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89dfd276-e745-53f7-bec4-dbb173a724c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343264" :question/problem "$$\n2x{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+xy=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $2x$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-\\frac{5}{2x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=\\frac{5}{2x}W(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6612a5c3-7365-59e4-af2c-8d05df31e370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367984} {:answer/id #uuid "00a0f95c-4557-5998-ab23-7ab0fe9a534a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367985} {:answer/id #uuid "25ac9f3b-47df-582a-8bc0-156ef48bcc1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367986} {:answer/id #uuid "067e7f21-cf9b-5eb2-9f75-690b906e1f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367987} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa7a9c7-af21-5be2-b29a-6810063b7d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ac9f3b-47df-582a-8bc0-156ef48bcc1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367986} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b5bd86f-1d21-509e-b9a3-bfc3fb18d87c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbe79ebb-dced-566b-a58c-a1fb18f2d4a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-343266" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by ${x}^{2}+1$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+1}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0fc3e51-7cae-577b-a0be-4881a008d40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367991} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f45fa4-5367-5e70-bf7e-45601e93674b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7f45fa4-5367-5e70-bf7e-45601e93674b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367992} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc3c9419-2dbe-5d02-a130-29d3fba4f698" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d3b6731-589f-5445-b353-7fd925ffc6cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343268" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-7y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=5.$ What is $W(3)?$\n\n$W(3)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 5 & =C \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5{e}^{-2\\cdot 3}=5{e}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da73127-b557-53c9-b0d0-0c0221dccf63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1da73127-b557-53c9-b0d0-0c0221dccf63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367995} :answer-field/id #uuid "57bfb22c-a9e3-5466-8464-fbb1f2696d4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c192ea08-e893-5ee6-88c7-eabdc991e52d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343272" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=2.$ What is $W(3)?$\n\n$W(3)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-5W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-5W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-5\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-5x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-5x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{-5\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{-5} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5\\cdot 3}=2{e}^{-10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7841cf-390f-57cb-a1d3-0168dd073520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c7841cf-390f-57cb-a1d3-0168dd073520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367998} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc92c137-b1f6-5798-ae2c-aa397ad863fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaa3d462-e129-5a72-b685-0d958f0b6d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-343279" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+(\\frac{2}{x}+1){y}^{′}+(2-x)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=3.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}}{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =3e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{3e}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{3e}{{2}^{2}}{e}^{-2}=\\frac{3}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acb9656d-f714-5f54-a52f-ef80e320cf40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acb9656d-f714-5f54-a52f-ef80e320cf40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368001} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bfc10f5-38bc-5d63-a026-9211b3baaeb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbcbc989-2062-54fc-9c57-039f60296a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343280" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=5.$ What is $W(\\pi )?$\n\n$W(\\pi )=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{1} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{-1}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\pi ) & =5{e}^{-1}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}=5{e}^{-1}{e}^{-1}=5{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd278087-ca94-5b5a-ac16-e490b0c9f661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368004} {:answer/id #uuid "8323690f-a235-505e-9a7b-65108d52eb7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd278087-ca94-5b5a-ac16-e490b0c9f661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368004} :answer-field/id #uuid "4df341e2-332a-5514-8bfd-57b58df35612" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "810dae74-735d-536c-8276-1e6046370c4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343281" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+(1+x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=1.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & ={x}^{3}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{{1}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =Ce \\\\ C & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)={e}^{-1}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & ={e}^{-1}{e}^{{2}^{3}}={e}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b68518c0-a075-5714-b075-8a30c9ba0694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{Q(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368008} {:answer/id #uuid "4f86af7a-77cc-50ad-9f2a-66595f1fa296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368009} {:answer/id #uuid "478d0a82-1f12-56e0-a31e-f2010e30814a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2xQ(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368010} {:answer/id #uuid "c9de8fff-e971-536e-826f-5b355371dbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f86af7a-77cc-50ad-9f2a-66595f1fa296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368009} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf765dee-c8b0-52bb-97f2-b7bd6534bbe2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "919dc07e-b443-5c96-85e0-24a9457ba489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368013} {:answer/id #uuid "64a0bfba-77b6-5ed6-b0b9-31becf94b95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368014} {:answer/id #uuid "812955fc-887d-54f6-8190-11ff99b2f0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368015} {:answer/id #uuid "c2025972-7109-505a-9272-c1a15b381210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2025972-7109-505a-9272-c1a15b381210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368016} :answer-field/id #uuid "62da4661-7019-59be-ab1b-896eb846737d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f79bb6f7-e5d1-5e88-a38d-a45e0502bad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368018} {:answer/id #uuid "63d0b04c-74a9-559d-b3f7-e71c3c5ffe22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368019} {:answer/id #uuid "8c79a98d-515e-5420-84f9-594f6ad77ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c79a98d-515e-5420-84f9-594f6ad77ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368020} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7207e9-2b8a-543e-8b6c-5ae4aaff6051" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4acb3708-3f85-57a1-975e-b6f23d95dbae" :question/math-academy-id "q-343293" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-2xW(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-2xW \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-2x\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-{x}^{2}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-{2}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{-4} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "139c2d00-5609-5fee-8109-53cda1a34cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{Q(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368023} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0c68fa-6c7c-58ca-ae8f-a0599b8846ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368024} {:answer/id #uuid "b8702eb2-7b5d-5b6b-9212-8b813cec1b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-Q(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368025} {:answer/id #uuid "0077df0a-c9c5-5cb1-a77c-9304a2a65a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0077df0a-c9c5-5cb1-a77c-9304a2a65a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368026} :answer-field/id #uuid "094c8c4f-9c46-5a96-9f9a-9b2971fc0c91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368022} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eaefd4b-d43f-5a9b-8f5f-5efe84cc