b/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4de0460-3f40-5061-8939-f952314d55b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-343294" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(\\pi )=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(\\pi )=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(\\pi )=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\pi ) & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b4f2d84-7f93-5152-b264-08ba32136ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368038} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eecf14-1a58-56f1-876b-14ad9f67ec0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368039} {:answer/id #uuid "048973b5-d2ee-5553-9953-7757a1ec567e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368040} {:answer/id #uuid "81956b6e-7def-5e08-bf2d-3583ff103a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81956b6e-7def-5e08-bf2d-3583ff103a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368041} :answer-field/id #uuid "4086dfcc-5ca5-5129-b69b-7487f8154af4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c05d24b9-c60d-5a5a-8305-3d6c7fab80bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368043} {:answer/id #uuid "f8eef7c6-eb23-5fe6-9405-b8d0d26de974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368044} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d88665-3945-5ca6-ab71-2c6f4d909b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368045} {:answer/id #uuid "9b754c45-93b6-5d8d-809f-0409a63719cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8eef7c6-eb23-5fe6-9405-b8d0d26de974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368044} :answer-field/id #uuid "4326316f-d3f2-53d6-aa07-5470293e093b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368042} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f86410-5e10-599d-bb4d-017d7b9fcb2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368048} {:answer/id #uuid "2c872a3d-64d9-58ef-8e78-38b325eb9723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368049} {:answer/id #uuid "bfad9185-365c-5c91-b8a0-e5e47cda4b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c872a3d-64d9-58ef-8e78-38b325eb9723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368049} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d9fb3c-78a9-5a52-9868-baf91d297c88" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b1f9810-dc22-5af5-9afc-b7fe4c62290d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343295" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 0} \\\\ 0 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ff6a52-f717-5a95-b6ff-5c77c8610102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368053} {:answer/id #uuid "38e43188-fffe-51f5-b9c2-e37d2feecd19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368054} {:answer/id #uuid "8924d964-e409-58ba-a397-b4cf4661fbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{Q(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368055} {:answer/id #uuid "2edfc57b-45c6-56a0-bf4f-925d5e947c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{Q(x)}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44ff6a52-f717-5a95-b6ff-5c77c8610102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368053} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f519f23-7d93-5719-a183-e7b57a7f1317" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c52951cf-e3fe-51b8-abdd-1d711b7a1389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368058} {:answer/id #uuid "44730857-5f2b-5ba3-95fb-9e2d10c89095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368059} {:answer/id #uuid "fa76adcd-fa8c-577a-a61a-b2e770586014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368060} {:answer/id #uuid "c37cd938-c23f-544a-beb1-211735c781f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44730857-5f2b-5ba3-95fb-9e2d10c89095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368059} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc1e296-0259-56ae-9666-643859836993" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cc34aa0-eff4-5988-bfae-bb40307695f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368063} {:answer/id #uuid "698b9c0c-2d28-5ce3-8dea-9f2ee4f7f010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368064} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b6d4f3-c9e0-5e50-abc3-95bbb8a8411e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "698b9c0c-2d28-5ce3-8dea-9f2ee4f7f010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368064} :answer-field/id #uuid "402e4563-e5e3-5183-bae8-fdf43d8669be" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcef6993-aa47-5f09-895e-379cbe321c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-343299" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{3}{x}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=5.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=5,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{3}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{3}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-3} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{C}{{2}^{3}} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{C}{8} \\\\ C & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x>0.$"}]}
